## With Pooling 

Math model: 
$$
\begin{align}
\texttt{surv}_i &\sim \text{Binomial}(N_i, p_i) \\ 
\text{logit}(p_i) &\sim \alpha_{\text{TANK}[i]} \\ 
\alpha_j &\sim \mathcal{N}(\bar{\alpha}, \sigma), j \in [48] \\ 
\bar{\alpha} &\sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1.5) \\ 
\sigma &\sim \text{Exponential}(1) 
\end{align}
$$

Here we will skip the prior predictive part and directly go into the model building part^[Note that the following corresponds to R code 13.3 in *Statistical Rethinking*]: 

:::{.panel-tabset group="language"}
# R 


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
m2 <- brm(data = df, family = binomial, 
    formula =  bf(surv | trials(density) ~ 1 + (1 | tank)), 
    prior = c(prior(normal(0, 1.5), class = Intercept), 
              prior(exponential(1), class = sd)), 
    cores = parallel::detectCores(), 
    seed = 51, 
    file=here("posts", "pymc-hierarchical", "m2.rds")
)
```
:::


To get a sense of the default priors that `brms` gave us: 

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
get_prior(bf(surv | trials(density) ~ 1 + (1 | tank)), family=binomial, data = df)
```

